<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hasan ibn Ali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 625–<a href="/years/670">670 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hasan ibn Ali was a grandson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> and the elder son of Ali, the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. After Ali's assassination in 661 he was briefly recognised as caliph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>. He then abdicated in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiya</a>, ending the Rashidun Caliphate and opening the way to Umayyad rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali.jpg" alt="Hasan ibn Ali" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Effective Light · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D8%B3%D9%86_%D8%A7%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%8A.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hasan ibn Ali was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in about 625, the firstborn of Muhammad's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> and Muhammad's cousin Ali. Hasan was raised in Muhammad's household until the prophet's death, when he was about seven, and early sources widely report Muhammad's love for him and his brother Husayn. He is part of the ahl al-bayt, Muhammad's family, and of the ahl al-kisa, which comprises Muhammad, Ali, Fatima and their two sons.</p><p>Hasan held no leading position under the first three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman. After Ali became caliph he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, where he raised an army of some 6,000 men, and Hasan fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-camel">Battle of the Camel</a> in 656 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a>, Talha and Zubayr.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Ali was assassinated by the Kharijite Abd al-Rahman ibn Muljam in January 661, Hasan was acknowledged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>. Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, did not recognise him and marched an army of sixty thousand men to Maskin, north of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, while urging Hasan in letters to give up his claim.</p><p>Hasan sent a vanguard under Ubayd Allah ibn al-Abbas, but he was severely wounded in an attempted assassination by a Kharijite, which demoralised his army and led to widespread desertion. Ubayd Allah and most of his troops also defected after Mu'awiya bribed him. In August 661 Hasan signed a peace treaty with Mu'awiya on the condition that Mu'awiya should rule in accordance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and the Sunnah, that a council should name his successor and that Hasan's supporters should receive an amnesty. He ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from January until August 661, then withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and did not engage in politics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hasan most likely died on 2 April 670. The early sources are nearly unanimous that he was poisoned, and Mu'awiya is usually identified as the instigator. His death removed an obstacle to the succession of Mu'awiya's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a>.</p><p>Critics have called the treaty with Mu'awiya a sign of weakness. His supporters hold that abdication was inevitable after his soldiers mutinied and that he sought unity and peace among Muslims, which a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> hadith says Muhammad had foretold. In Shia Islam Hasan is the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a>, who succeeded Ali and was followed by his brother Husayn, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> are viewed as separate institutions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hasan ibn Ali” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Into this country came Idris ibn Abdallah, a great-grandson of <b>Hasan ibn Ali</b> and so a descendant of Ali ibn Abi Talib and Fatima, the daughter of the prophet Muhammad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hasan ibn Ali</b>’s subsequent abdication in favour of Muawiya ended the Rashidun principle of consultative selection and began the hereditary Umayyad Caliphate, which changed Islamic government from its experimental early phase into a dynastic imperial model.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Abbasid forces crushed an Alid revolt led by al-Husayn ibn Ali, a descendant of <b>Hasan ibn Ali</b>, near Mecca; al-Husayn and many of his followers were killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-11">11 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 967 Ja'far ibn Muhammad, a descendant of al-<b>Hasan ibn Ali</b>, took control of Mecca as the Fatimid Caliphate rose in Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/sharifate-of-mecca">Sharifate of Mecca</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 866 Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Ukhaydir, a descendant of al-<b>Hasan ibn Ali</b>, fled the Hejaz after a failed revolt and took control of al-Yamama in Najd.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukhaydirid-emirate">Ukhaydirid Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=654" aria-label="Medina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5603 1263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hasan ibn Ali"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6053 1533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6053 1533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=654">What was here in 654 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
